@meta
id: 21-12
title: Until there is nothing left to find
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-recursive
par: 70
minutes: 8
vscode: find next, edit twice, find next, seven times
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
`OrderProvider` still logs with `console.log`. The platform team wants
every call on the structured logger, with the payload wrapped:

    console.log('cancel', id);    →    this.logger.info('cancel', { id });

Seven calls, spread through the file, each with a different message
and a different single-word argument.

Record a macro whose first move is the search for `console.log(` and
whose last move is `@a`. The search finds the next call wherever it
is; the edit removes the thing being searched for; the recursion runs
until the search comes up empty.

@keys
| `/console\.log(` | the next call, anywhere below, wrapping to the top |
| `cf(` | change up to and including the bracket |
| `f,` `w` | onto the comma, then onto the argument |
| `e` | to the end of the argument |
| `@a` at the end | run again |

@hints
`qaq` first. Then `qa`, the search, and the edit: `cf(this.logger.info(`
`Esc` replaces the call name and the bracket. `f,w` puts the cursor on
the argument; `i{ ` `Esc`, `ea }` `Esc` wrap it. Then `@a`, `q`.
--
The search has to fail eventually or the macro never stops. It does,
because each edit turns `console.log(` into `this.logger.info(`, and
the last search finds nothing at all.
--
`qaq` then `qa/console\.log(<Enter>cf(this.logger.info(<Esc>f,wi{ <Esc>ea }<Esc>@aq`, then `@a`.

@solution
    qaq   qa /console\.log( <Enter> cf( this.logger.info( <Esc> f,w i{  <Esc> e a } <Esc> @a q   @a   :wq<Enter>

Sixty-two keystrokes. Twelve of them are the search, and the search is
what makes this one different from the last lesson.

A recursive macro over consecutive lines ends when a guard fails on
the first line past the data. A recursive macro driven by a search
ends when the search fails, which happens exactly when no unedited
target remains anywhere in the file. That is a strong guarantee. It
does not matter where the calls are, how far apart, or whether one of
them is above where you started: the search wraps round and finds it.

The guarantee holds only because the edit consumes the match. If the
pattern still matched after the edit, the search would keep succeeding
for ever and the macro would go round the file until you pressed
`Ctrl-C`. So before you type the final `@a` in a recording that starts
with `/`, ask: after this edit, does the pattern still match this
line? If it does, either change the pattern or use a count.

The `\.` in the pattern is chapter 13: a bare `.` would match any
character, which here would be harmless, and elsewhere would not.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    protected logger: Logger,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    console.log('findOne', id);
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      console.log('missing', id);
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    console.log('cancel', id);
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      console.log('refused', order);
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
    console.log('refunded', order);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    console.log('markShipped', order);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    console.log('findByCustomer', customerId);

    return this.orderRepository.findMany({ where: { customerId } });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    protected logger: Logger,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    this.logger.info('findOne', { id });
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      this.logger.info('missing', { id });
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    this.logger.info('cancel', { id });
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      this.logger.info('refused', { order });
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
    this.logger.info('refunded', { order });

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    this.logger.info('markShipped', { order });

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    this.logger.info('findByCustomer', { customerId });

    return this.orderRepository.findMany({ where: { customerId } });
  }
}
